WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.722
Welcome back. So far, you have considered the lens

2
00:00:03.744 --> 00:00:07.700
you bring, used community context to prepare questions, and

3
00:00:07.722 --> 00:00:11.389
looked at communication and access. Now let us bring

4
00:00:11.411 --> 00:00:14.989
those ideas into one conversation: how do we make

5
00:00:15.011 --> 00:00:17.011
room for the person who will live with the

6
00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:17.500
decision?

7
00:00:18.290 --> 00:00:22.579
Our continuing example is a planning opportunity. A division

8
00:00:22.601 --> 00:00:25.468
team knows what the opportunity is for and when

9
00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:28.157
a decision is due. It is still working out

10
00:00:28.179 --> 00:00:31.312
how to explain the invitation, how people can respond,

11
00:00:31.334 --> 00:00:35.001
and what the responsible decision maker can consider. No

12
00:00:35.023 --> 00:00:38.423
single format or personal preference has been decided in

13
00:00:38.446 --> 00:00:38.890
advance.

14
00:00:39.524 --> 00:00:42.836
A person-centered approach does not mean the staff member

15
00:00:42.858 --> 00:00:46.769
can promise every requested change. It means the person

16
00:00:46.791 --> 00:00:50.347
has a meaningful chance to understand the opportunity, say

17
00:00:50.836 --> 00:00:53.747
what matters to them, and take part in ways

18
00:00:53.769 --> 00:00:56.480
that work for them. Your role is to make

19
00:00:56.502 --> 00:01:00.102
the process clear, listen, and follow through on what

20
00:01:00.124 --> 00:01:00.813
you can do.

21
00:01:01.403 --> 00:01:05.048
When a requirement or decision belongs to another office,

22
00:01:05.381 --> 00:01:07.426
say so and identify the right route.

23
00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:12.166
Imagine a participant says they would like a trusted

24
00:01:12.188 --> 00:01:15.277
supporter to join a conversation and would also like

25
00:01:15.299 --> 00:01:18.854
some private time before responding. The supporter might be

26
00:01:18.877 --> 00:01:22.743
a relative, friend, advocate, or another person the participant

27
00:01:22.766 --> 00:01:26.677
chooses. Their relationship alone does not tell you what

28
00:01:26.699 --> 00:01:28.766
role the participant wants them to have.

29
00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:32.399
You might begin: “Would you like your supporter here

30
00:01:32.421 --> 00:01:35.354
for the whole discussion, or for part of it?

31
00:01:35.599 --> 00:01:38.443
What would you like to talk through privately?” Give

32
00:01:38.466 --> 00:01:41.421
the person time to answer in their preferred way.

33
00:01:41.621 --> 00:01:46.021
Speak to them directly, provide appropriate communication support, and

34
00:01:46.043 --> 00:01:49.510
avoid directing every question to the supporter merely because

35
00:01:49.532 --> 00:01:51.043
that person is present.

36
00:01:51.890 --> 00:01:55.179
Ask what the supporter is invited to do. They

37
00:01:55.201 --> 00:01:58.601
may help the person prepare questions, provide information the

38
00:01:58.623 --> 00:02:01.734
person wants shared, or offer another kind of support.

39
00:02:02.268 --> 00:02:04.512
Do not assume they make the decision. If a

40
00:02:04.534 --> 00:02:07.801
legal representative or another formal arrangement may affect the

41
00:02:07.823 --> 00:02:11.534
process, follow the applicable procedure and seek the appropriate

42
00:02:11.557 --> 00:02:15.112
guidance. Explain the roles that actually apply without guessing

43
00:02:15.134 --> 00:02:16.601
from a relationship or label.

44
00:02:18.079 --> 00:02:21.234
A good invitation makes it easier to decide whether

45
00:02:21.257 --> 00:02:24.457
and how to take part. Explain the purpose in

46
00:02:24.479 --> 00:02:27.634
plain language, the time available, what is still open

47
00:02:27.657 --> 00:02:30.768
to influence, and how a person can respond. Ask

48
00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:35.034
what language, format, communication support, timing, or other access

49
00:02:35.057 --> 00:02:39.057
arrangements would be helpful. Arrange support through current procedures

50
00:02:39.079 --> 00:02:41.190
and check whether it works for the person.

51
00:02:41.903 --> 00:02:45.526
When the participant asks for private time, discuss the

52
00:02:45.548 --> 00:02:49.814
available options and the timing honestly. For example, the

53
00:02:49.837 --> 00:02:51.992
person might want a break during a meeting or

54
00:02:52.014 --> 00:02:54.948
time afterward to consider an accessible copy of the

55
00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:58.948
information. Check which arrangements are available and whether there

56
00:02:58.970 --> 00:03:00.103
is a decision deadline.

57
00:03:00.793 --> 00:03:04.327
If you cannot offer the requested timing, explain the

58
00:03:04.349 --> 00:03:07.927
constraint and seek an appropriate alternative rather than presenting

59
00:03:07.949 --> 00:03:09.971
the limitation as the person's fault.

60
00:03:10.982 --> 00:03:14.316
Be clear about privacy without making promises you cannot

61
00:03:14.338 --> 00:03:18.027
keep. Explain how information will be used and any

62
00:03:18.049 --> 00:03:22.204
applicable limits, using current guidance. For this learning activity,

63
00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:24.760
you do not need to record a participant's name,

64
00:03:25.116 --> 00:03:28.804
health information, case details, or reasons for choosing support.

65
00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:31.716
A general note such as “check the available private

66
00:03:31.738 --> 00:03:33.404
response options” is enough.

67
00:03:34.416 --> 00:03:38.038
Consider the participant who wants a trusted supporter at

68
00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:42.193
the conversation and private time before responding. Three possible

69
00:03:42.216 --> 00:03:45.682
responses are available on this page. Choose the one

70
00:03:45.704 --> 00:03:48.482
you would take first, then explore the feedback. You

71
00:03:48.504 --> 00:03:50.304
can pause the video while you decide.

72
00:03:52.129 --> 00:03:55.507
Consider what each response would mean for the participant

73
00:03:55.529 --> 00:03:59.018
and the planning process. Once you make a choice,

74
00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:01.129
the feedback will explain its effects.

75
00:04:02.263 --> 00:04:06.419
Think of a meeting, notice, or planning conversation connected

76
00:04:06.441 --> 00:04:09.397
to your work. Where does the process let a

77
00:04:09.419 --> 00:04:12.663
person say whom they want involved? Where could it

78
00:04:12.686 --> 00:04:15.819
make private thinking time or another response route clearer?

79
00:04:15.841 --> 00:04:19.619
If you are not responsible for the process, identify

80
00:04:19.641 --> 00:04:21.886
a question to bring to someone who is.

81
00:04:22.402 --> 00:04:26.558
Write a general action rather than personal information. You

82
00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:28.869
might note: “Ask how the person wants a supporter

83
00:04:28.891 --> 00:04:32.758
involved, confirm available private response options, and explain the

84
00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:35.958
decision deadline.” If a question involves a specific case

85
00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:40.447
or protected information, use your organization's approved channels instead

86
00:04:40.469 --> 00:04:41.469
of these learning notes.

87
00:04:42.636 --> 00:04:46.258
The existing CLAS learning and planning worksheet offer ways

88
00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:51.191
to consider communication, access and participation together. The access

89
00:04:51.213 --> 00:04:53.436
checks in the Library can help you examine whether

90
00:04:53.458 --> 00:04:56.858
a meeting, information, or response route can actually be

91
00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:00.324
used. These resources support your judgment; follow current program

92
00:05:00.347 --> 00:05:02.480
procedures and the person's stated needs.

93
00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:05.758
In the next part, we will step back from

94
00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:09.491
one conversation and look at the process itself. A

95
00:05:09.513 --> 00:05:12.847
respectful interaction matters, and so does the way the

96
00:05:12.869 --> 00:05:16.647
notice, deadline, meeting, and response options work together for

97
00:05:16.669 --> 00:05:18.091
people who encounter them.
